---
import CaseStudy from "~/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseHero from "~/components/case/CaseHero.astro";
import CaseLead from "~/components/case/CaseLead.astro";
import Closing from "~/components/case/Closing.astro";
import Gotchas from "~/components/case/Gotchas.astro";
import HomelabDiagram from "~/components/case/HomelabDiagram.astro";
import MemoryBar from "~/components/case/MemoryBar.astro";
import NextNav from "~/components/case/NextNav.astro";
import Scenes from "~/components/case/Scenes.astro";
import SceneStep from "~/components/case/SceneStep.astro";
import Snippet from "~/components/case/Snippet.astro";
import Section from "~/components/ui/Section.astro";
import { apps, ways } from "~/lib/art";
import appSnippet from "~/content/snippets/argocd-application.html?raw";

const icons = ["si-argo", "si-tailscale", "si-cloudflare", "si-traefikproxy", "si-letsencrypt"] as const;
---

<CaseStudy
  type="article"
  title="Homelab · Guus Ekkelenkamp"
  og="homelab"
  description="Case study: a single-node k3s platform with Argo CD and Home Assistant, and no open ports."
  icons={icons}
>
  <CaseHero
    project="homelab"
    meta="Personal · 2026"
    title="Homelab"
    line="The patterns I use at work, on one old laptop under the TV."
  />
  <CaseLead>
    <p>
      I like tinkering, and I don’t like recommending things at work that I haven’t run myself. So an old
      laptop under the TV, lid shut, became the place where I try them first. It also hosts my side projects,
      like the MTG tools my friends use.
    </p>
    <p>
      What makes it different from most home servers is how things get onto it. It runs the same git-driven
      delivery we use for client projects: I push to main and the new version shows up without me touching the
      box. It has its own domain and real certificates, and nothing on it is reachable from the internet
      unless I open it on purpose.
    </p>
  </CaseLead>
  <Scenes id="deploy" title="How a change gets deployed">
    <SceneStep scene="loop" range={[0, 0.35]} caption="One change, from git push to a running pod.">
      <h3>From git push to running</h3>
      <p>GitHub Actions builds every commit into an image on GHCR, tagged with the commit SHA.</p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="loop" range={[0.35, 0.63]} continues>
      <p>
        Argo CD Image Updater notices the new image, commits its version back into the app’s repo, and Argo CD
        syncs. The commit is the deploy record, so git always says what’s running.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="loop" range={[0.63, 0.76]} continues>
      <p>
        At work, production only follows release tags. At home, main deploys directly. I skip that step on
        purpose, because here the only user I can annoy is me.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="loop" range={[0.76, 1]} continues>
      <h3>The loop</h3>
      <p>
        That commit back into the repo once caught me out. CI built the commit, made a new image, and Image
        Updater committed again, in a loop. CI now ignores <code>deploy/</code> with <code>paths-ignore</code>
        .
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
  </Scenes>
  <Section id="repo" title="One repo runs it all" bodyClass="prose">
    <p>
      One repo holds an app-of-apps root that manages every app, including Argo CD itself. Adding an app is
      adding one file, and one shared credential lets Argo CD read all my private repos. It runs 15
      applications now.
    </p>
    <HomelabDiagram svg={apps()} kind="apps" />
    <Snippet
      caption="An Argo CD Application and its Image Updater config"
      label="Argo CD Application and ImageUpdater for one app"
      code={appSnippet.trimEnd()}
    />
  </Section>
  <Section id="getting-in" title="Three ways in" bodyClass="prose">
    <p>
      The tailnet is the default. My own devices reach Traefik over Tailscale, and DNS for my domain points at
      the tailnet for everything except this site, so those names only lead somewhere from my devices.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two other ways in are opt-in, per app. A few apps are public through a Cloudflare Tunnel with Access in
      front, so there’s a login and no open port. This site is the exception: it’s public with no login. For a
      party, Tailscale Funnel gives a temporary public URL, for a quiz night, say. Every app in the cluster
      gets TLS from one wildcard Let’s Encrypt certificate, issued through Cloudflare DNS-01.
    </p>
    <HomelabDiagram svg={ways()} kind="ways" />
  </Section>
  <Section id="box" title="Inside the box" bodyClass="prose">
    <p>
      The laptop has 16 GB of memory. Proxmox gives 10 GB to an Ubuntu VM with k3s and 3 GB to Home Assistant;
      the host keeps about 2 GB. The battery doubles as a UPS, which is the best job that battery has had in
      years.
    </p>
    <MemoryBar
      label="Memory split: 10 GB k3s, 3 GB Home Assistant, about 2 GB host, about 1 GB spare"
      parts={["10 GB k3s", "3 GB Home Assistant", "~2 GB host", "~1 GB spare"]}
    />
    <p>
      I run k3s in a VM because Kubernetes in an LXC container needs privileged mode and tends to break on
      upgrades. I take a snapshot before every upgrade, and everything else can be rebuilt from git. One node
      means no redundancy, so the next step is three second-hand mini PCs.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="home-assistant" title="Home Assistant" bodyClass="prose">
    <p>
      Home Assistant runs in its own VM next to the cluster, because passing the Zigbee USB stick through to a
      VM is one line of config. HTTPS goes through an nginx proxy, with plain HTTP on the home network as a
      fallback.
    </p>
    <p>
      I moved it from a Raspberry Pi with a full backup restore, and the whole Zigbee mesh came across without
      re-pairing a single device. The dashboards and automations live in git, and a tablet on the wall shows
      the dashboard.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="lessons" title="Lessons" bodyClass="prose">
    <Gotchas
      items={[
        {
          symptom: "Builds kept running in a loop, each one producing a new image (August 2026).",
          cause: "Image Updater’s own commit started a new build.",
          fix: "Make CI ignore <code>deploy/</code> with <code>paths-ignore</code>.",
        },
        {
          symptom: "The k3s node hit disk pressure at about 30 GiB, on a 64 GiB virtual disk.",
          cause: "The Ubuntu installer gave root only about half of the disk.",
          fix: "Extend the root volume into the free space with <code>lvextend</code> and <code>resize2fs</code>. If the volume group has no free space left, first grow the disk in Proxmox (<code>qm resize</code>), then run <code>growpart</code> and <code>pvresize</code> inside the guest.",
        },
        {
          symptom: "Public DNS stopped resolving on the Linux VMs on my tailnet.",
          cause: "Tailscale MagicDNS was on without a global nameserver.",
          fix: "Set a global nameserver in Tailscale’s DNS settings.",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Section>
  <Closing
    line="Most of what I’ve learned here started with something I broke at home first, which is exactly what it’s for."
    stack="Built with: Proxmox, k3s, Argo CD and Image Updater, GitHub Actions, GHCR, cert-manager, Traefik, Tailscale, Cloudflare and Home Assistant."
  />
  <NextNav
    prev={{
      href: "/projects/hosting-platform/",
      small: "Previous project",
      label: "&larr; Hosting platform",
    }}
    next={{ href: "/projects/mtg-suite/", small: "Next project", label: "MTG suite &rarr;" }}
  />
</CaseStudy>
